  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nConvection associated with an upstream prefrontal trough is\nlargely fading early this morning, leaving remnant convective\ncloud debris drifting into the forecast area. This cloud cover,\ncombined with a modest tightening of the surface pressure\ngradient ahead of the approaching trough, will limit radiational\ncooling and help keep river valley fog at bay through daybreak\nacross the northwestern half of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe overall severe thunderstorm threat for this afternoon and \nevening has been tempered due to poorer quality low-level \nmoisture pooling ahead of the surface cold front. Guidance that \ntypically trends moist, such as WRF-based models, only pools \nsurface dewpoints in the lower 70s, while high-resolution HRRR \nand MOS guidance suggest afternoon mixing into the mid to upper \n60s. With surface high temperatures reaching the mid to upper \n80s across the lowlands, MLCAPE values will reach 1000 to 1500 \nJ/kg. However, modest convective inhibition and drier boundary \nlayer air raise questions regarding overall convective coverage,\nespecially with the best surface convergence along the front\ngenerally lagging the best ribbon of instability. \n\nDespite uncertain coverage, any deep moist convection that\nsuccessfully initiates will be capable of localized severe\nhazards. Deep-layer 0-6km bulk shear of 20 to 25KTs will \nsupport small convective clusters and perhaps a transient \nsupercellular storm structure from a split. Boundary layer \nprofiles display steep low-level lapse rates between 7.5 and 8.5\nC/km and large dewpoint depressions below H700, yielding DCAPE \nvalues of 800 to 1000 J/kg. \n\nA marginally severe hail report wouldn't be out of the question,\nalthough wet bulb zeros are rather high given the generally weak\ninstability and shear, would likely require a left split to\nproduce a large enough stone in cloud to carry severe potential\nto the ground. \n\nSPC does continue to advertise a marginal risk for both hazards,\nalthough forecast continuity does seem to be an increasing\nfactor for maintaining this. Any storm activity that does form \nwill diminish rapidly between 8 PM and 10 PM EDT with nocturnal \ncooling and the frontal passage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nUpper-level troughing will linger near the East Coast on \nFriday, driving a reinforcing and largely dry cold front across \nthe region. Cooler northwesterly flow will hold Friday high \ntemperatures in the lower 80s across the lowlands and 70s in the\nmountains. \n\nHeading into the weekend and early next week, an amplifying\nupper-level ridge centered over the central country will shift\neastward and anchor over the Ohio Valley and central\nAppalachians. Forecast guidance keeps the region firmly\nsuppressed under the core of the ridge, keeping any shortwave\ndisturbances or ridge-rider convective complexes well to the\nnorth across the Great Lakes through at least the middle of next\nweek. \n\nSensible weather will turn increasingly hot for late August and\nearly September. Lowland daytime highs will reach the upper 80s\non Saturday and rise into the lower 90s Sunday through Tuesday.\nDewpoints mixing into the low and mid 60s during peak afternoon\nheating will keep heat index values near actual air\ntemperatures, keeping conditions below heat headline criteria.",
